# RuangNode Cline Rules

## Project identity

Project name: RuangNode Domain: ruangnode.me

RuangNode is a commercial digital-product marketplace and managed
application hosting platform.

## Primary engineering rule

Build for the intended full architecture.

Do not intentionally create a disposable MVP, fake backend, mock
provisioning system, or cosmetic resource system unless a task
explicitly requests a mock/test implementation.

## Architecture boundaries

Keep these responsibilities separate:

- Web UI
- Customer panel
- Admin panel
- Control Plane API
- Database
- Payment providers
- Provisioning engine
- Node scheduler
- Node Agent
- Docker runtime

The frontend must never directly control Docker.

The public internet must never receive unrestricted Docker socket
access.

## Multi-tenant security

Every customer-owned resource must be authorized against the
authenticated user.

Never trust: - user IDs from request bodies - instance IDs without
ownership checks - product IDs for authorization - filenames - paths -
webhook payloads without verification

Prevent: - IDOR - path traversal - secret leakage - cross-tenant data
access - host escape - arbitrary host command execution

## Resource isolation

Resource limits must be enforced by infrastructure.

Never implement a UI-only limit.

Managed instances should use appropriate container controls such as: -
CPU limits - memory limits - swap policy - PID limits - storage limits -
network controls when supported

The Node Agent is responsible for applying and verifying resource
limits.

## Node Agent

The Node Agent runs on infrastructure nodes.

It may: - create containers - delete containers - start/stop/restart
containers - update resource limits - collect metrics - retrieve logs -
perform health checks - perform explicitly authorized file operations -
provide controlled console functionality

It must not expose arbitrary host shell access to customers.

All control-plane-to-agent communication must be authenticated.

Prefer an outbound/secure agent connection model where practical so the
control plane does not require an openly exposed privileged Docker API.

## Secrets

Never commit: - API keys - passwords - private keys - payment secrets -
database passwords - session secrets - Cloudflare tokens - provider
credentials

Use environment variables or a proper secret-management mechanism.

Never print secrets to logs.

## Database

Use strong relational modeling.

Keep these concepts distinct:

User Product ResourceProfile Order Payment Subscription Node Instance
ProvisioningJob Credential Ticket AuditLog

Do not collapse all service state into one giant table.

## Payments

Payment providers must be adapters.

Do not couple the order system directly to one provider.

Always verify webhook signatures.

Never mark an order paid solely because the browser returned from a
checkout page.

## Provisioning

Provisioning must be idempotent.

Repeated webhook delivery must not create duplicate instances.

Track provisioning state explicitly.

Recommended state flow:

PENDING → PAID → PROVISIONING → ACTIVE

Failure states must be recoverable.

## UI

Use the RuangNode Neo-Brutalist design system.

Required visual language: - black borders - hard shadows - high
contrast - bold typography - electric blue - purple - pink - yellow -
cyan - green - generous whitespace

Avoid generic SaaS styling.

Do not replace the design with glassmorphism or a default dashboard
template.

## UX

The customer should understand: - what they bought - what resources they
receive - where the service is running - whether it is online - what it
is using - how to configure it - how to renew/upgrade - how to get
support

## TypeScript

Prefer: - strict typing - explicit interfaces/types - schema validation
at boundaries - small modules - dependency injection for infrastructure
adapters - testable services

Avoid: - `any` unless justified - giant files - global mutable state -
hidden side effects

## API

API handlers should: 1. authenticate 2. authorize 3. validate input 4.
execute service logic 5. return a stable response

Never put business logic directly into UI components.

## Errors

Errors should be: - structured - safe for users - detailed in server
logs - free of secrets

Do not expose stack traces in production.

## Testing

Important areas require tests: - authorization - resource ownership -
pricing/order calculations - webhook verification - provisioning
idempotency - resource profile application - Node Agent authentication -
tenant isolation

## Dependencies

Do not add a dependency merely because it is convenient.

Before introducing a new major dependency: - verify it solves a real
architectural need - consider maintenance - consider security - consider
bundle/runtime cost

## Documentation

When architecture changes, update the corresponding documentation.

Important decisions must not exist only in chat history.

## Coding workflow

Before implementing a major feature: 1. inspect the current architecture
2. identify affected boundaries 3. update types/schema if necessary 4.
implement backend/domain logic 5. implement API 6. implement UI 7. add
tests 8. update documentation

Do not silently redesign unrelated subsystems.

## Completion criteria

A task is not complete merely because the UI renders.

For infrastructure-backed features, verify the real behavior.

For example, a resource limit feature is incomplete if it only displays
“2 GB” without enforcing a 2 GB container limit.

## Brand

Always use: - RuangNode - ruangnode.me

Do not rename the project to NexOps or another previous project name.
